        "thinking": "Good. Now I see the flow. The `registerCfExtraRoutes(app)` is called BEFORE the `/api/*` catch-all. Since Hono matches routes in registration order, the extra routes (join-requests, skills, etc.) registered there will match FIRST and return before the catch-all is reached.\n\nSo if I now add `accessRoutes(db, opts)` routes via the bridge to `route-registry.ts` and those routes overlap with `cf-extra-routes.ts` routes (like `/api/companies/:id/join-requests`), the Hono routes in `cf-extra-routes.ts` will still win because they're registered first.\n\n\nFor routes that exist in `access.ts` but not in `cf-extra-routes.ts`, the bridge-based handlers will catch them in the catch-all, which is what we want. But there's a problem with the `compiledPathCache` — when routes are rebuilt on each request with fresh DB closures, the cache gets reused by index even though the route set might have changed, especially now that we're pulling routes from `accessRoutes` via `extractAllRoutesFromRouter`.\n\nThe key insight is that `extractAllRoutesFromRouter` walks the router stack and returns one definition per method-path combination, so the count stays consistent since `accessRoutes` always registers the same routes. The bridge creates fresh closures each time that capture the current DB instance through the Express router services, which is correct. But the path cache relies on routes being in the same order, and since `accessRoutes` registers routes deterministically, the order should be stable — though this assumption needs verification.\n\nThe cache invalidation problem only matters during development when the route count might change; in production the worker bundle is static and restarts clear the module-level cache anyway. For the duplicate join-requests route, the Hono version in `cf-extra-routes.ts` takes precedence since it's registered before the `/api/*` catch-all, so the bridge-based version would only be reached if the Hono route didn't match.\n\nNow I'm wondering if there could be route conflicts within the catch-all itself—the compiled routes array might contain duplicate entries for the same method and path.\n\nLooking at the potential overlaps, the company CRUD routes from `route-registry.ts` and the `accessRoutes` paths like `/api/companies/:id/members` are actually distinct, so there shouldn't be collisions there. But I'm realizing the bridge handlers already wrap the Express middleware chain with auth checks like `assertCompanyAccess`, and the catch-all is also calling `resolveActorFromRequest` before invoking the handler, so I need to think through whether that creates redundant or conflicting middleware execution. ockReq` sets `req.actor = ctx.actor`. So the Express handlers will see the correct actor.\n\nBut wait - the bridge creates Express-style handlers that re-do their own auth. For example, `assertCompanyAccess(req, companyId)` in access.ts checks `req.actor`. Since `buildMockReq` sets `req.actor = ctx.actor`, this should work correctly.\n\nOK, I think the implementation is correct. Let me now also think about the `companySkillRoutes` - it registers routes like `GET /companies/:companyId/skills/:skillId`, `DELETE /companies/:companyId/skills/:skillId`, `POST /companies/:companyId/skills`, and `PATCH /companies/:companyId/skills/:skillId/...`. But `cf-extra-routes.ts` already has `GET /api/companies/:companyId/skills` as the list endpoint, so I need to check for path collisions between the Hono route and the catch-all routes from the bridge. The Hono route in `cf-extra-routes.ts` would take precedence for the list endpoint, while the detail, delete, and create routes would be handled by the catch-all.\n\nFor the detail, delete, and create routes, they'd call `companySkillService`, which uses `ensureSkillInventoryCurrent` that requires `fs.stat`. The FS shim would throw at runtime, causing a 500 error, but the worker wouldn't crash. I should look more closely at what `companySkillService` actually does to see which methods depend on the filesystem.",
